{#- The formatter standard the generated C already satisfies.

    This file is emitted, not merely documented, because the acceptance
    property is idempotence: running `clang-format --style=file --dry-run
    -Werror` over the generated `.c`/`.h` next to it must report nothing. A
    downstream consumer that reformats the sources with a different style would
    produce a diff against the artifact the manifest checksums, so the style
    has to travel with the code.

    `ColumnLimit: 0` is the load-bearing choice. Generated expressions are
    machine-sized — a single fused covariance update can be a thousand columns
    — and any finite limit would make the formatter re-wrap them, which both
    destroys the emitter's own line structure and makes the output depend on
    the clang-format version. With no limit the formatter normalizes
    indentation, spacing and brace placement and preserves every line break the
    emitter chose. Nothing here is deprecated in any clang-format from 14
    onward, so the check is portable across the versions a project is likely to
    pin. -#}
# clang-format configuration for rumoca-generated GALEC C ({{ algorithm_code.block_name }}).
# The generated sources already conform: `clang-format --style=file --dry-run
# -Werror *.c *.h` reports no replacements. Reformatting with any other style
# invalidates the checksums recorded in the manifest.
---
Language: Cpp
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
ColumnLimit: 0
IndentWidth: 4
ContinuationIndentWidth: 4
UseTab: Never
AlignAfterOpenBracket: DontAlign
BreakBeforeBraces: Attach
PointerAlignment: Right
SpaceAfterCStyleCast: false
AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: None
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: Never
AllowShortLoopsOnASingleLine: false
AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: Never
IndentCaseLabels: true
MaxEmptyLinesToKeep: 1
SeparateDefinitionBlocks: Always
SortIncludes: Never
ReflowComments: false
AlignTrailingComments: false
AlignConsecutiveAssignments: false
IndentPPDirectives: None
